---
/**
 * `/app/search/syntax` — what you can type into the search box.
 *
 * This page exists because the search became *strict* (ADR-0031). It used to
 * run every query with `fuzziness: AUTO`, which spent two character edits on
 * every word without being asked: searching for `compare` returned `compact`
 * and `company`, each exactly two edits away. That is a bad trade in a legal
 * corpus, where a different word is a different rule — so the default now
 * matches the words as typed.
 *
 * The cost of that is real and this page is how it is paid: a reader who *was*
 * relying on the looseness — because they are unsure of a spelling — needs to
 * be told how to ask for it back. Hence `~1`, and hence a guide rather than a
 * silent behaviour change.
 *
 * It documents both halves of the one box, because the box is one box: what it
 * does with your words, and what it accepts as a citation. Those used to be
 * two controls and are not any more (BUILDLOG 027), so a guide that covered
 * only the keyword half described a control that no longer exists — and the
 * citation half was the one nobody could have guessed at, since "type a
 * citation in more or less any written form" is a claim about 84 accepted
 * shapes with nothing on the site listing them.
 *
 * Both tables are data, in `lib/searchsyntax.ts` and `lib/citationforms.ts`,
 * and both are checked against the code that implements them:
 * `tests/test_search_syntax.py` against the `simple_query_string` flags
 * `api/search.py` enables, and `tests/test_citation_forms.py` by running every
 * documented example through `citeparse.parse_citation` and comparing the
 * identifier it produces with the one this page prints.
 */
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { CITATION_FORMS, CITATION_LIMITS } from "../../lib/citationforms";
import { SEARCH_OPERATORS } from "../../lib/searchsyntax";
import { setPublicCache } from "../../lib/cache";
import { APP, gotoHref, searchHref } from "../../lib/url";

/** Static prose about a parser — it changes when the code does, not per
 * request, and never per reader. Revalidate rather than pin: there is no
 * release point to make it immutable against. */
setPublicCache(Astro.response.headers, null, false);

/** One query shown two ways, so the label form and the date form are visibly
 * the same request. `119-99` is a real release point and `06/12/2026` is its
 * currency date, which is what makes the pair a demonstration rather than two
 * unrelated examples. */
const RELEASE_EXAMPLE = {
  query: "conservation",
  release: "119-99",
  date: "06/12/2026",
};
---

<Base title="Search and citation guide · U.S. Code">
  <h1 class="doc-title">Search and citation guide</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    There is one box in the header and it answers two kinds of question. Type a citation
    and it goes straight there; type anything else and it searches the text. You do not
    have to tell it which you meant — it reads what you wrote.
  </p>

  <p>
    Both halves can also ask about the past. A citation can name a release point and land
    on the provision as it stood then; a search can name one in the box with{" "}
    <code>release:</code> or <code>date:</code>, or on the results URL. See{" "}
    <a href="#release">searching at a release point</a>.
  </p>

  <nav class="apidocs__contents" aria-label="On this page">
    <p class="apidocs__contents-item"><a href="#citations">Going to a citation</a></p>
    <p class="apidocs__contents-item"><a href="#operators">Searching the text</a></p>
    <p class="apidocs__contents-item"><a href="#release">Searching at a release point</a></p>
  </nav>

  <h2 class="toc-group" id="citations">Going to a citation</h2>

  <p>
    Each of these forms works, and each is followed by the official identifier it resolves
    to. Select any example to try it.
  </p>

  <ul class="syntax-list">
    {
      CITATION_FORMS.map((form) => (
        <li class="syntaxop">
          <p class="syntaxop__head">
            <span class="syntaxop__name">{form.name}</span>
          </p>
          <p class="syntaxop__try">
            <a href={gotoHref(form.example)}><code>{form.example}</code></a>
            <span class="syntaxop__arrow" aria-hidden="true"> → </span>
            <span class="usa-sr-only"> goes to </span>
            <code class="syntaxop__result">{form.identifier}</code>
          </p>
          <p class="syntaxop__what">{form.explanation}</p>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <h3 class="toc-group">Two things that will catch you out</h3>

  <ul class="syntax-list">
    {
      CITATION_LIMITS.map((limit) => (
        <li class="syntaxop">
          <p class="syntaxop__head">
            <code class="syntaxop__syntax">{limit.example}</code>
            <span class="syntaxop__name">
              {limit.identifier === null ? "not read as a citation" : "parses, finds nothing"}
            </span>
          </p>
          <p class="syntaxop__what">{limit.explanation}</p>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <p class="doc-meta">
    Add a release point to any of these — <code>?release=119-99</code> or{" "}
    <code>?date=07/12/2026</code> — and you land on the provision as it stood then.
  </p>

  <h2 class="toc-group" id="operators">Searching the text</h2>

  <p class="lede">
    By default a search matches the words you typed, and every one of them has to
    appear. The operators below are how you loosen that deliberately — including
    asking for near-spellings when you are not sure how a word is written.
  </p>

  <div class="usa-summary-box" role="region" aria-labelledby="strict-heading">
    <div class="usa-summary-box__body">
      <h2 class="usa-summary-box__heading" id="strict-heading">Strict by default</h2>
      <div class="usa-summary-box__text">
        <p>
          Searching for <code>compare</code> finds provisions containing the word
          <em>compare</em> — not <em>compact</em> or <em>company</em>. This site used to
          return all three, because it ran every search with a two-character-edit
          tolerance nobody asked for. In a body of law a different word is a different
          rule, so that tolerance is now something you request.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you are unsure of a spelling, add <code>~1</code> to the word:{" "}
          {/* No whitespace inside the anchor: a newline around the `<code>`
              becomes an underlined trailing space hanging off the link. */}
          <a href={searchHref("conservation~1")}><code>conservation~1</code></a> finds
          provisions containing anything within one character edit of it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 class="toc-group">Operators</h3>

  {/* A list, not a table.
      This was a four-column table — write / to / example / what it does — and
      a table is arguably the honest markup for it. But one of those columns is
      a paragraph of prose, and in a ~700px reading measure the other three
      left it about 185px: every row became eight lines tall, and on a phone
      the whole thing had to scroll sideways. The operator, its example and its
      explanation are one thing said three ways, so they are one block. */}
  <ul class="syntax-list">
    {
      SEARCH_OPERATORS.map((operator) => (
        <li class="syntaxop">
          <p class="syntaxop__head">
            <code class="syntaxop__syntax">{operator.syntax}</code>
            <span class="syntaxop__name">{operator.name}</span>
          </p>
          <p class="syntaxop__what">{operator.explanation}</p>
          <p class="syntaxop__try">
            <a href={searchHref(operator.example)}><code>{operator.example}</code></a>
          </p>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <h3 class="toc-group">What is searched</h3>

  <p>
    The text of sections and the headings above them — chapters, subchapters, parts. A
    search covers the law <strong>currently in force</strong> unless you name a release
    point, described <a href="#release">in the next section</a>.
  </p>

  <p class="doc-meta">
    Operators combine, and grouping works the way it does in arithmetic:{" "}
    <a href={searchHref("(forest | grassland) -timber")}><code>(forest | grassland) -timber</code></a>
    .
  </p>

  <h3 class="toc-group">Ordering the results</h3>

  <p>
    Results come back by relevance. <code>&amp;sort=</code> on the results URL takes{" "}
    <code>relevance</code>, <code>citation</code> — the Code's own order, title by title —
    or <code>recent</code>, newest text first by the release point at which each provision's
    current text first appeared. The <strong>Order</strong> control above the results sets
    the same parameter.
  </p>

  <h2 class="toc-group" id="release">Searching at a release point</h2>

  <p class="lede">
    You can search the Code as it stood at a past release point.
  </p>

  <p>
    Type <code>release:</code> or <code>date:</code> into the search box, or add the same
    two parameters a citation URL takes to the results URL: the label of a release point,
    or a date. When both name a release point, the parameter wins.
  </p>

  <ul class="syntax-list">
    <li class="syntaxop">
      <p class="syntaxop__head">
        <code class="syntaxop__syntax">&amp;release=</code>
        <span class="syntaxop__name">by release point</span>
      </p>
      <p class="syntaxop__what">
        The label as the Office of the Law Revision Counsel writes it, awkward ones
        included: <code>119-102not101</code> is current through Public Law 119-102{" "}
        <em>except</em> 119-101. The <a href={`${APP}/releases`}>release points page</a>{" "}
        lists them all.
      </p>
      <p class="syntaxop__try">
        <a href={searchHref(RELEASE_EXAMPLE.query, { release: RELEASE_EXAMPLE.release })}>
          <code>?q={RELEASE_EXAMPLE.query}&amp;release={RELEASE_EXAMPLE.release}</code>
        </a>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="syntaxop">
      <p class="syntaxop__head">
        <code class="syntaxop__syntax">&amp;date=</code>
        <span class="syntaxop__name">by date</span>
      </p>
      <p class="syntaxop__what">
        <code>MM/DD/YYYY</code>, resolved to the release point in force on that day. Usually
        easier than looking up a label. The results page names the release point it landed
        on, so you can see which one answered.
      </p>
      <p class="syntaxop__try">
        <a href={searchHref(RELEASE_EXAMPLE.query, { date: RELEASE_EXAMPLE.date })}>
          <code>?q={RELEASE_EXAMPLE.query}&amp;date={RELEASE_EXAMPLE.date}</code>
        </a>
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>

  {/* The limit, as it now stands. It is a summary box rather than a footnote
      because a reader who does not see it will read an incomplete answer as a
      complete one — and a search that quietly under-reports is worse in a legal
      corpus than a search that refuses.

      This box used to say the index held current text alone, which was true
      when it was written and stopped being true on 2026-08-02, when the
      superseded pass finished on the box (`docs/deploy-status.md`). It sat here
      telling readers the site could not do something it does. That is the
      failure ADR-0038 exists to prevent, and this is the sentence that
      prompted it. */}
  <div class="usa-summary-box" role="region" aria-labelledby="pit-heading">
    <div class="usa-summary-box__body">
      <h3 class="usa-summary-box__heading" id="pit-heading">
        What a search at a release point covers
      </h3>
      <div class="usa-summary-box__text">
        <p>
          The index holds <strong>every version of every section</strong>, not only the
          text in force — 489,578 documents, of which 423,649 are superseded text. A
          section amended in 2024 can be searched as it read in 2019, and the answer comes
          from the text as it stood then rather than from today's.
        </p>
        <p>
          One known gap, and it is small. Where the source publishes several elements under
          a single <code>@identifier</code> at a single release point, they share one
          document and the index keeps one of them — 160 documents across the whole corpus,
          9 of them in current text. Those provisions are complete in the reader; it is the
          search index that holds one of the pair.
        </p>
        <p>
          You can always tell which text you searched: the results page says either{" "}
          <em>“In the text as of release point …”</em> or{" "}
          <em>“Searching the law currently in force.”</em>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 class="toc-group">Asking what cites a provision</h3>

  <p>
    Prefix a citation with <code>cites</code> — <a href={gotoHref("cites 26 usc 501")}
      ><code>cites 26 usc 501</code></a
    > — and the search looks for provisions that refer to it. This is a keyword search
    over the citation's text for now, not a real cross-reference index, and the results
    page says so rather than letting the answer pass for the finished thing.
  </p>
</Base>
